# a bunch of important key-facts to keep in mind in regards to dietary habits



>> Vitamins
! There are 2 kinds: water-soluble, and fat-soluble
! It's unlikely for you to overdose on vitamins through food consumption, even at high amounts; supplements are more dangerous
! The limits and needs vary per person, the following are general recommendations

> Water-soluble vitamins
* C
    * Recommended:  90 mg ;  Upper-limit:  2000 mg
    - Overdosing can cause gastrointestinal disturbances
* B1 (thiamine)
    * Recommended:  1.2 mg
    + No observed toxicity
* B2 (riboflavin)
    * Recommended:  1.3 mg
    + No observed toxicity
* B3 (niacin)
    * Recommended:  16 mg ;  35 mg
    - Overdosing can cause liver damage
* B5 (pantothenic acid)
    * Recommended:  5 mg
    + No observed toxicity
* B6 (pyridoxine)
    * Recommended:  1.3 mg ;  Upper-limit:  100 mg
    - Overdosing can cause nerve damage, also symptoms: skin lesions, sensitivity to light, heartburn
* B7 (biotin)
    * Recommended:  30 mcg
    + No observed toxicity
* B9 (folate / folic acid)
    * From food: dark leafy greens, avocado, beans, citrus
    * Recommended:  400 mcg ;  Upper-limit:  1000 mcg
    - Overdosing can cause affected mental function, negatively impact immune system, mask severe B12 deficiency
* B12 (cobalamin)
    * Recommended:  2.4 mcg
    + No observed toxicity?
    + Unlike other vitamins, it can be stored for years in the liver, so it's easier to prevent deficiency
        - Though its absorption requires the protein intrinsic

> Fat-soluble Vitamins (easier to overdose)
* A (retinol / retinyl esters)
    ! affects vision
    * From food: meat, eggs, oily fish, dairy, carotenoid plants (including green leafy vegetables), tomatoes, carrots, mango
    * Recommended:  900 mcg retinol activity equivalents ;  Upper-limit:  3000 mcg retinol activity equivalents
    - Overdosing can cause nausea, increased intracranial pressure, coma, and death
* D
    ! Affects immune system, bone/hair loss
    * From food: fatty fish, egg yolks. Also from 15 minutes of sunlight exposure.
    * Recommended:  600 mg ;  Upper-limit:  4000 mg
    - Overdosing can cause blood clotting, appetite loss, hemorrhages, stroke (hemorrhagic), irregular heartbeat, raised blood calcium levels (causes organ damage)
* E
    * From food:  plant oils (soy / corn / olive), nuts, seeds, wheat germ
    * Recommended:  15 mg ;  Upper-limit:  1000 mg
    - Overdosing can cause blood clotting, nausea/vomitting, diarrhea, headache
* K
    ! Affects proper blood clotting and reduces the chance of heart disease
    ! K1 and K2 serve about the same purposes
    * K1 from food: green vegetables; cooked kale / turnip-greens / collard-greens / spinach , raw dandelion-greens / arugula (rocket), dried herbs you use as seasoning
    * K2 from food: meats/fish
    * Recommended:  120 mcg
    + No observed toxicity



>> Minerals

* Magnesium
    ! Regulates muscle and nerve function, balances blood sugar levels, involved in building proteins / bone / DNA
    * From food: pumpkin, spinach, artichoke, soybeans / tofu, brown rice, nuts
    * Recommended:  400 mg ;  Upper-limit:  ???
        * Magnesium that's bonded with oxygen is water soluble, and filtered out through the kidneys
    - Overdosing can cause diarrhea, nausea/vomiting, depression, low blood pressure, heart attack

* Calcium
    ! Building block for bones and teeth
    * Recommended:  1000 mg ;  Upper-limit:  ???
    * From food: pumpkin seeds, chia seeds, almonds, milk/cheese/yogurt, salty fish, broccoli/kale, potato, nuts, beans/lentils
    
* Zinc
    ! Involved in healing wounds, dealing with stress, usage of nutrients
    * Recommended:  15-30 mg ;  Upper-limit:  40 mg
    * From food: pumpkin seeds, spinach, oats, tahini, sardines, brown rice, tempeh, organ meats
    
* Iron
    * Recommended:  18 mg
    


>> Fats / Oils
! Related to the high density of energy in oil/fat: the human brain is 60% fat
! Canola oil is basically repurposed machine-lubrication oil
    ! Same category of seed-oils (vegetable oils): Sunflower/Soybean/Grape-seed/Corn/...-oil.
    ! Not only as oil; crystallized cotton is used as lard, which is similarly harmful

! omega-3 fatty acids deteriorate when heated/cooked, so eating raw (or closer to raw) fish is actually beneficial


> DHA (Docosahexaenoic Acid, omega-3)
! High amounts of DHA significantly contribute to brain development, and health of other organs too
* Found in all kinds of sea-food (fish and algae), like salmon, sardines, seaweed

> EPA (Eicosapentaenoic Acid, omega-3)
! Lowers risk of cardiovascular disease and helps control cholesterol levels, and reduces mood disorders
* Found in all kinds of sea-food (fish and algae)
    * Quality deteriorates when heated/cooked
    * Eating 2 sardines means consuming a tablespoon of animal fats

> ALA (Alpha-Linolenic Acid, omega-3)
* Converted (inefficiently) into EPA and DHA in the body
    * can be found in flaxseeds, chia seeds, walnuts...

> AA (Arachidonic Acid, omega-6)
! Essential for brain function, muscle growth and repair
! Precursor to pro-inflammatory molecules (eicosanoids)
* Found in meat, eggs, poultry

> LA (Linoleic Acid, omega-6); avoid!
! Competes with DHA, AA, EPA
! Can't be avoided entirely. Harmful at its quantities in today's food industry; you need to minimize consumption of it
* Found in vegetable fats/oils (AVOID!!!)
    * A tablespoon of vegetable oil is produced from 560 sunflower seeds, or 70,000 canola seeds
        * As opposed to consuming a tablespoon of animal fats, which can be obtained from eating 2 sardines 
* Olives are naturally oily; LA is only a small portion of an olive's fats. The overall fats composition of olives is healthy (same for olive oil)

> Oleic Acid (omega-9)
! improves cardiovascular health: lowers cholesterol levels, reduces blood pressure
! anti-inflammatory, antioxidant (protects cells from damage)
! Non-essential (the body can produce omega-9). Doesn't mean unimportant.
* Found in olives (and olive-oil), avocado, nuts

> Saturated fats (minimize!)
! Excessive intake is associated with heart disease!
* Fount in animal products (meat, butter, dairy), coconut oil, palm oil



>> Fasting
! There are many views on fastering, and various ways to fast

> Preserving muscles during fast
! Your body needs to produce keytones to protect your muscles
* If you don't have much body-fat, you'll lose muscle faster
* Exercise (in moderation) keeps the body from dismantling muscles
    * Simply doing long walks is enough to preserve all muscles
* Avoid eating carbohydrates before fasting, because they delays keytosis (causing a sooner break-down of muscle)
* Eat proteins and enough calories after fasting 
* Drink water, but also keep up your electrolytes (salt, potassium, magnesium)




